// Pathfinder Run custom pedestrian model.
//
// Implements the static path-type weighting AND greenness/park-proximity
// boost from docs/running-app-architecture.md §5, point 1's first two
// bullets: prefer dedicated pedestrian infrastructure, penalize (not
// exclude) unprotected major roads, exclude anything without foot access,
// and prefer edges inside a park/green-space polygon.
//
// Deliberately NOT included here (separate, later work):
// - Edge-reuse penalty (app-level logic in the route generator, not a
//   GraphHopper profile concern)
// - The live closures penalty (per-request only -- see generate_loop.py's
//   fetch_return_leg/fetch_outbound_leg -- because it changes request to
//   request, unlike greenness, which is permanent once the graph is built)
//
// Greenness (`in_greenspace`): sourced from data/greenspace/greenspace.geojson
// (built by scripts/build_greenspace.py from this extract's leisure=park,
// natural=wood, landuse=forest, leisure=nature_reserve,
// landuse=recreation_ground, and leisure=garden ways/relations -- checked
// against this specific extract, not assumed: 774 leisure=park ways alone,
// including real named parks like Waterloo Park). Loaded via config.yml's
// `custom_areas.directory`, GraphHopper's STATIC per-import mechanism, not
// the per-request `areas` used for closures/long-way buffering -- greenness
// is a permanent routing preference (the doc lists it alongside path-type
// weighting as an edge-weight factor, §5 point 1, not a per-candidate
// scoring term, §5 point 3), so it belongs baked into the graph, not
// evaluated per request. Confirmed empirically against the running server
// that this is fully CH/speed-mode compatible -- a plain GET with no
// ch.disable logged `dijkstrabi|ch-routing`, not a flexible-mode fallback --
// unlike every other custom_model mechanism built so far, which all require
// ch.disable because they're per-request.
//
// 1.3x is a deliberately modest boost, chosen to protect against a primary/
// secondary road that happens to pass through or border a park getting
// boosted into out-competing a plain sidewalk/footway elsewhere. Verified
// on a real case in this extract, not just the arithmetic in the abstract:
// Westmount Road North (a real SECONDARY road, way 38896608) crosses a
// greenspace polygon for part of its length. Isolated each road/greenness
// combination via a real request confined to a single way (weight/distance
// ratio, lower = cheaper):
//   FOOTWAY, not in greenspace (baseline):      0.480
//   SECONDARY, not in greenspace:                2.400  (0.480/2.400 = 1/5,
//                                                 exactly matches 1.5/0.3)
//   SECONDARY, IN greenspace (Westmount Rd N):   1.864  (2.400/1.864 = 1.287,
//                                                 matches the 1.3x boost)
// The boosted secondary road (1.864) is still ~3.9x MORE expensive than a
// plain footway elsewhere (0.480) -- confirms the road-class tier keeps
// dominating regardless of greenness, exactly as intended.
//
// Note: this replaces the stock model's `multiply_by: foot_priority` rather than
// layering on top of it. GraphHopper's built-in `foot_priority` (FootPriorityParser)
// already does its own road-class weighting AND reads the literal `sidewalk=*` tag
// on a way directly -- a signal not exposed as any encoded value queryable here, so
// this explicit model can't distinguish "primary road with a mapped sidewalk" from
// "primary road with none." Keeping both would double-penalize/boost the same
// categories unpredictably, so this is an explicit tradeoff: visible, tunable rules
// here, at the cost of that sidewalk-tag signal until it's reintroduced deliberately.
//
// TRUNK/MOTORWAY get their own (lower) tier rather than sharing PRIMARY/SECONDARY's:
// the stock model penalizes fast/limited-access roads far harder than ordinary
// arterials (REACH_DESTINATION = 0.1x vs BAD = 0.5x for primary/secondary), and this
// keeps that same margin rather than flattening it.
{
  "priority": [
    { "if": "!foot_access", "multiply_by": "0" },
    { "if": "road_class == PATH || road_class == FOOTWAY || road_class == PEDESTRIAN || road_class == LIVING_STREET", "multiply_by": "1.5" },
    { "else_if": "road_class == PRIMARY || road_class == SECONDARY", "multiply_by": "0.3" },
    { "else_if": "road_class == TRUNK || road_class == MOTORWAY", "multiply_by": "0.1" },
    { "if": "in_greenspace", "multiply_by": "1.3" }
  ],
  "speed": [
    { "if": "true", "limit_to": "foot_average_speed" }
  ]
}
